WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.382 --> 00:00:05.320
- I never imagined in my
wildest dreams that I would be

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:06.290
successful.

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:07.348
You live where you are.

00:00:07.348 --> 00:00:10.864
I think actors live
exactly where they are.

00:00:10.864 --> 00:00:11.920
The really good ones.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.087
Empathy is the engine that
powers all the best in us.

00:00:17.719 --> 00:00:19.812
I don't really have a
bag of tricks to go to

00:00:19.812 --> 00:00:21.645
or a method, you know?

00:00:22.621 --> 00:00:23.454
I don't.

00:00:23.454 --> 00:00:27.570
There are some days when I
myself think I'm overrated.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.788
- She's an American actress
cited in the media as the

00:00:29.788 --> 00:00:32.296
best actress of her generation.

00:00:32.296 --> 00:00:34.417
She's known for the
versatility in her roles,

00:00:34.417 --> 00:00:36.488
adaptations in the
characters that she plays and

00:00:36.488 --> 00:00:39.267
be able to take on multiple accents.

00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:41.989
She's one of only six actors
to have won three or more

00:00:41.989 --> 00:00:45.082
competitive Academy Awards for acting.

00:00:45.082 --> 00:00:47.240
She's Meryl Streep, and
here's my take on her top

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:48.877
10 rules for success.

00:00:48.877 --> 00:00:51.173
Number 10 is my personal
favorite, and I'm curious to

00:00:51.173 --> 00:00:54.673
find out which one you guys like the most.

00:00:56.972 --> 00:01:00.785
- I never imagined in
my wildest dreams that I

00:01:00.785 --> 00:01:02.452
would be successful.

00:01:04.905 --> 00:01:05.738
- [Crew Member] Role play.

00:01:05.738 --> 00:01:06.841
- [Director] Action.

00:01:06.841 --> 00:01:08.722
- [Meryl] The fear is
always there with actors.

00:01:08.722 --> 00:01:11.424
That you'll never work again.

00:01:11.424 --> 00:01:12.257
Help.

00:01:12.257 --> 00:01:13.674
Help me, help me.

00:01:16.132 --> 00:01:18.205
That goes with the territory.

00:01:18.205 --> 00:01:21.688
Oh gee, where am I going to go now?

00:01:21.688 --> 00:01:23.692
You have that fear, the
second job, third job,

00:01:23.692 --> 00:01:26.500
fourth job, you have it
all the way down the line,

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:30.667
so each happy event, as it's
happened has been a surprise.

00:01:33.554 --> 00:01:34.695
- I'm not out of date am I?

00:01:34.695 --> 00:01:37.770
Picking flowers for a woman
as a sign of appreciation?

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.298
- No, not at all, except
those are poisonous.

00:01:40.298 --> 00:01:42.277
(laughs)

00:01:42.277 --> 00:01:44.854
I'm Damacles with the sword,
you know, I really think

00:01:44.854 --> 00:01:46.854
it's up there by a hair.

00:01:48.238 --> 00:01:50.985
And every day it doesn't
happen I'm just thrilled.

00:01:50.985 --> 00:01:51.818
Yeah!

00:01:52.806 --> 00:01:54.921
I never thought it was
going to happen to me.

00:01:54.921 --> 00:01:56.782
You can't if you're an actor.

00:01:56.782 --> 00:01:59.247
I'm really freaking here
Johnny, why is he here?

00:01:59.247 --> 00:02:02.560
Some people do, I guess, go
in saying I'm going to be

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.727
a star, and they are, but
for all those people that say

00:02:08.282 --> 00:02:10.316
that I bet there are hundreds
of thousands who say that

00:02:10.316 --> 00:02:12.399
and are still struggling.

00:02:14.274 --> 00:02:15.107
- Where you going?

00:02:15.107 --> 00:02:16.191
- I don't know.

00:02:16.191 --> 00:02:19.235
I know a lot of my success
has to do with luck.

00:02:19.235 --> 00:02:21.646
I've also poured a lot
of hard work into it,

00:02:21.646 --> 00:02:25.563
so I feel in some way
that I can justify myself

00:02:27.157 --> 00:02:30.983
but I'm just happy that
things have happened this way.

00:02:30.983 --> 00:02:33.733
I know about you and Thelma Rice.

00:02:34.983 --> 00:02:36.058
I know everything.

00:02:36.058 --> 00:02:37.196
It's all here.

00:02:37.196 --> 00:02:38.029
- Shit.

00:02:38.029 --> 00:02:39.224
- You didn't even have
the decency to hide the

00:02:39.224 --> 00:02:40.724
evidence, you just

00:02:42.031 --> 00:02:44.069
threw it in the drawer.

00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:45.236
Hotel, motels.

00:02:47.043 --> 00:02:48.162
- Aw, shit.

00:02:48.162 --> 00:02:51.205
- You couldn't even pay cash
like a normal philanderer.

00:02:51.205 --> 00:02:53.488
You charged everything,
I mean look at this.

00:02:53.488 --> 00:02:56.038
Flowers, look at all these
flowers that you bought for her!

00:02:56.038 --> 00:02:58.638
And you occasionally brought
me home a bunch of wilted

00:02:58.638 --> 00:03:02.553
zinnias, how can you do
this if I'm such a vixen?

00:03:02.553 --> 00:03:03.916
Tell me.

00:03:03.916 --> 00:03:05.916
- Is what you do an art?

00:03:08.081 --> 00:03:09.248
Is it a craft?

00:03:11.625 --> 00:03:12.916
Is it a job?

00:03:12.916 --> 00:03:17.083
- You mean is it high falutin
or mid falutin or a paycheck?

00:03:19.453 --> 00:03:21.373
Yeah, I don't know.

00:03:21.373 --> 00:03:23.760
When other people do it,
sometimes when I watch other

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.496
people do it really well,
I think it's beyond an art

00:03:27.496 --> 00:03:29.413
it's like making music.

00:03:31.137 --> 00:03:32.844
- But at its best for you?

00:03:32.844 --> 00:03:35.914
- But at its best it's
like flying, it's great.

00:03:35.914 --> 00:03:38.060
It's great, and that's the
part I wouldn't give up

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:40.880
and that's the part I
won't give up even though

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.047
I love my children and being
home and doing what they

00:03:46.494 --> 00:03:47.443
need me to do.

00:03:47.443 --> 00:03:52.409
I certainly also need to
express myself in this way.

00:03:52.409 --> 00:03:55.186
I don't expect you to understand
but you must know that

00:03:55.186 --> 00:03:57.436
Jonas has become dangerous.

00:03:58.345 --> 00:03:59.890
He must be stopped.

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:03.573
He must not get beyond
the triangle of rocks.

00:04:03.573 --> 00:04:05.542
- What do you want me to do?

00:04:05.542 --> 00:04:07.326
- You know, Jonas.

00:04:07.326 --> 00:04:09.770
I want you to find him.

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:12.437
And then I want you to lose him.

00:04:14.055 --> 00:04:17.273
- How are actors different
than the rest of us?

00:04:17.273 --> 00:04:20.426
- Well, they live a zen life.

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:22.895
It's very uncertain.

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:27.577
And all lives are uncertain,
but actors know it.

00:04:27.577 --> 00:04:32.072
And actors, because you're
unemployed so often,

00:04:32.072 --> 00:04:34.785
and you live so intensely
in the moments that you are

00:04:34.785 --> 00:04:35.868
working, that

00:04:42.114 --> 00:04:46.281
when you come back to earth and
look around and that balloon

00:04:48.164 --> 00:04:51.338
has gone, and there's no other one on

00:04:51.338 --> 00:04:54.505
the horizon so you live where you are.

00:04:55.622 --> 00:04:59.212
I think actors live
exactly where they are.

00:04:59.212 --> 00:05:00.601
The really good ones.

00:05:00.601 --> 00:05:02.349
And that's why they seem kind of crazy.

00:05:02.349 --> 00:05:03.599
- But we all should be
there, isn't that where we

00:05:03.599 --> 00:05:04.787
should all want to be?

00:05:04.787 --> 00:05:05.753
- But there really ...

00:05:05.753 --> 00:05:07.011
Yes, I think so.

00:05:07.011 --> 00:05:09.322
I think it's an authentic way to live.

00:05:09.322 --> 00:05:10.634
I am an optimist.

00:05:10.634 --> 00:05:11.670
I believe in the future.

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:13.525
And people who do are the
ones who make history instead

00:05:13.525 --> 00:05:14.856
of just sitting around
- [Male] Ellie.

00:05:14.856 --> 00:05:15.909
watching it.

00:05:15.909 --> 00:05:17.978
No they're willing to take the big risks.

00:05:17.978 --> 00:05:19.514
Yes, I made a decision.

00:05:19.514 --> 00:05:20.347
Oh, god.

00:05:21.668 --> 00:05:23.001
Where are all the men anymore?

00:05:23.001 --> 00:05:26.247
My father Tyler Prentiss
never asked, "Is this okay?

00:05:26.247 --> 00:05:27.497
"Is this okay?"

00:05:28.391 --> 00:05:30.486
You know what I'm saying, Mark?

00:05:30.486 --> 00:05:32.594
He just did what needed to be done.

00:05:32.594 --> 00:05:34.875
- You said that in trying
to find a character you look

00:05:34.875 --> 00:05:38.080
for what comes out of the eyes.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.247
- I think I meant that in
connection to working ...

00:05:43.779 --> 00:05:47.461
I don't feel like I exist
until I'm with someone

00:05:47.461 --> 00:05:49.662
else.
- [Interviewer] A partner?

00:05:49.662 --> 00:05:50.558
Yeah.

00:05:50.558 --> 00:05:52.242
- How important is listening?

00:05:52.242 --> 00:05:53.470
- It's everything.

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:57.031
And it's where you learn everything.

00:05:57.031 --> 00:05:59.698
And I always think of acting ...

00:06:04.492 --> 00:06:06.481
when I was applying to law
school and thinking, "Well,

00:06:06.481 --> 00:06:11.137
"acting is a stupid way
to make a living, and it

00:06:11.137 --> 00:06:12.765
"doesn't do anything in the world."

00:06:12.765 --> 00:06:13.812
But I think it does.

00:06:13.812 --> 00:06:14.993
I think it does.

00:06:14.993 --> 00:06:17.380
I think there's a great
worth in it and the worth is

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:19.547
in listening to people who

00:06:24.442 --> 00:06:26.275
maybe don't even exist

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:32.359
or who are voices in your
past, and through you

00:06:32.359 --> 00:06:35.615
come through the work and you
give them to other people.

00:06:35.615 --> 00:06:39.963
And I think that giving
voice to characters that have

00:06:39.963 --> 00:06:43.698
no other voice is a ...
that's the great worth of

00:06:43.698 --> 00:06:44.615
what we do.

00:06:45.626 --> 00:06:47.815
Because so much of acting is vanity.

00:06:47.815 --> 00:06:49.024
So much of this is ...

00:06:49.024 --> 00:06:51.784
I mean this feels so great
to come out here and sit here

00:06:51.784 --> 00:06:56.234
and have everybody clap,
but the real thing that

00:06:56.234 --> 00:06:59.234
makes me feel so good is when I know

00:07:00.448 --> 00:07:03.031
I've said something for a soul,

00:07:06.282 --> 00:07:08.939
you know I've presented a soul.

00:07:08.939 --> 00:07:09.772
- Ah!

00:07:13.524 --> 00:07:14.441
Ah!

00:07:14.441 --> 00:07:15.423
- Yes!

00:07:15.423 --> 00:07:16.256
No!

00:07:16.256 --> 00:07:17.222
Oh, damn.

00:07:17.222 --> 00:07:18.231
This is pointless.

00:07:18.231 --> 00:07:19.064
Wait a minute.

00:07:19.064 --> 00:07:20.461
This is ridiculous, we
can't even hurt each other.

00:07:20.461 --> 00:07:22.270
We can't even inflict pain.

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:23.811
- Pain?

00:07:23.811 --> 00:07:26.567
I'll tell ya about pain!

00:07:26.567 --> 00:07:30.567
- I've thought a lot about
the power of empathy.

00:07:31.962 --> 00:07:36.255
In my work it's the current
that connects me and my

00:07:36.255 --> 00:07:40.401
actual pulse to a fictional
character in a made up

00:07:40.401 --> 00:07:41.234
story.

00:07:41.234 --> 00:07:45.655
It allows me to feel
pretend feelings and sorrows

00:07:45.655 --> 00:07:47.155
and imagined pain.

00:07:48.514 --> 00:07:52.431
And my nervous system is
sympathetically wired,

00:07:54.905 --> 00:07:57.427
and it conducts that
current to you, sitting in a

00:07:57.427 --> 00:07:58.594
movie theater.

00:07:59.638 --> 00:08:03.984
And to the woman sitting next
to you, and to her friend

00:08:03.984 --> 00:08:07.902
so that we all feel that
it's happening to us at

00:08:07.902 --> 00:08:08.778
the same time.

00:08:08.778 --> 00:08:11.771
It's a very mysterious and
valuable resource of the

00:08:11.771 --> 00:08:12.938
human species.

00:08:14.686 --> 00:08:18.519
And women I think access
it most effortlessly.

00:08:19.508 --> 00:08:24.185
We cry at sad movies, we
don't feel we lose face or

00:08:24.185 --> 00:08:26.602
stature or position doing it.

00:08:28.118 --> 00:08:30.459
We see a news story that enrages us and we

00:08:30.459 --> 00:08:35.099
write letters through
tears, our hearts pounding.

00:08:35.099 --> 00:08:39.266
I've often, I used to wonder
why human beings developed

00:08:40.297 --> 00:08:44.130
these inconvenient and
embarrassing responses,

00:08:45.109 --> 00:08:48.526
this sniffling, choking, wet obstruction.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:51.870
(laughs)

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:54.688
You know, the thing that
physicians and soldiers,

00:08:54.688 --> 00:08:58.992
and stock traders and
journalists and fashion models,

00:08:58.992 --> 00:09:01.720
and politicians, and news
commentators, and venture

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:04.628
capitalists all must suppress
in order to work most

00:09:04.628 --> 00:09:05.593
efficiently.

00:09:05.593 --> 00:09:07.676
(laughs)

00:09:08.914 --> 00:09:11.414
I thought what possible value,

00:09:12.982 --> 00:09:16.190
function could it serve
in the Darwinian scheme of

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:18.788
survival of the fittest and
the strongest and the most

00:09:18.788 --> 00:09:19.955
heavily armed.

00:09:22.518 --> 00:09:26.856
No seriously, I thought
why and how did we evolve

00:09:26.856 --> 00:09:30.439
with this weak, and
useless passion in tact

00:09:33.710 --> 00:09:36.127
within the deep heart's core.

00:09:38.418 --> 00:09:42.118
And the answer as I've
formulated it to myself is that

00:09:42.118 --> 00:09:46.285
empathy is the engine that
powers all the best in us.

00:09:49.029 --> 00:09:51.029
It is what civilizes us.

00:09:52.824 --> 00:09:55.049
It is what connects us.

00:09:55.049 --> 00:09:57.129
Every woman needs makeup.

00:09:57.129 --> 00:09:58.869
Don't let anybody tell you different.

00:09:58.869 --> 00:10:01.284
The only woman pretty
enough to go without makeup

00:10:01.284 --> 00:10:04.617
was Elizabeth Taylor and she wore a ton.

00:10:05.951 --> 00:10:08.907
Your shoulders are all slumped
and your hair's all straight

00:10:08.907 --> 00:10:09.740
and you don't wear makeup.

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:11.255
You look like a lesbian.

00:10:11.255 --> 00:10:12.148
- Mom.

00:10:12.148 --> 00:10:14.118
- Do you continue to learn?

00:10:14.118 --> 00:10:14.965
- [Meryl] Yeah, sure.

00:10:14.965 --> 00:10:15.798
- Do you really?

00:10:15.798 --> 00:10:16.631
- Yeah.

00:10:16.631 --> 00:10:19.120
- In other words, having
done this experience you

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:20.037
are better.

00:10:21.836 --> 00:10:23.019
- I don't know, I don't know.

00:10:23.019 --> 00:10:23.936
This was so

00:10:26.928 --> 00:10:28.216
fun.

00:10:28.216 --> 00:10:30.897
And sort of effortless
that it, it didn't ...

00:10:30.897 --> 00:10:34.372
I think you learn more from
the challenging things,

00:10:34.372 --> 00:10:36.753
things that are tougher to do.

00:10:36.753 --> 00:10:38.131
In the past.

00:10:38.131 --> 00:10:40.391
- What was the toughest?

00:10:40.391 --> 00:10:41.590
- Oh.

00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:42.618
- Sophie's Choise or?

00:10:42.618 --> 00:10:43.451
- No.

00:10:43.451 --> 00:10:44.368
No, no, no.

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:48.431
There have been tough things
that I care I probably

00:10:48.431 --> 00:10:51.431
won't go into, but just because they

00:10:54.058 --> 00:10:58.225
my molecules change in me,
according to how happy I am

00:11:02.636 --> 00:11:04.803
and my creativity gets ...

00:11:07.656 --> 00:11:11.739
What I learn every time
is how to wrangle all the

00:11:12.757 --> 00:11:16.411
elements that make me
love what I do and make it

00:11:16.411 --> 00:11:18.744
sort of happen effortlessly.

00:11:19.942 --> 00:11:23.426
And when that doesn't come
easily I don't really have

00:11:23.426 --> 00:11:27.765
a bag of tricks to go to
or a method, you know?

00:11:27.765 --> 00:11:28.918
I don't.

00:11:28.918 --> 00:11:33.085
So I come unmoored, and part
of that is a very good thing

00:11:34.003 --> 00:11:37.021
because you have to reassemble.

00:11:37.021 --> 00:11:38.495
Nobody knows what I'm talking about.

00:11:38.495 --> 00:11:39.328
- No we do!

00:11:39.328 --> 00:11:42.127
I'm sitting here thinking ...
- Actors do.

00:11:42.127 --> 00:11:43.346
- So, no, no, no.

00:11:43.346 --> 00:11:47.419
- And so it's very good to
have to start blank and figure

00:11:47.419 --> 00:11:49.509
out how to begin again.

00:11:49.509 --> 00:11:52.426
How to begin again, it's very good.

00:11:53.452 --> 00:11:57.561
- Methinks the right, honorable
lady doth screech too much!

00:11:57.561 --> 00:11:59.644
(laughs)

00:12:01.434 --> 00:12:04.100
- If the right, honorable
gentleman could perhaps attend

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:08.110
more closely to what I'm
saying rather than how I am

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:12.277
saying it he may receive a
valuable education in spite

00:12:13.772 --> 00:12:14.689
of himself.

00:12:16.678 --> 00:12:20.845
Many many of my friends woke
up at 3 years of age and

00:12:22.135 --> 00:12:24.360
said, "I have to be on the stage."

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:27.165
And I've never, never had that.

00:12:27.165 --> 00:12:29.675
I've always been an omnivore.

00:12:29.675 --> 00:12:33.240
Interested in way too many
things and I actually found

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:37.000
the one profession that
fed all my appetites.

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:40.333
- What is it that you really like to do?

00:12:41.295 --> 00:12:42.601
- Eat.

00:12:42.601 --> 00:12:44.684
(laughs)

00:12:45.852 --> 00:12:46.685
That's what I like to do!

00:12:46.685 --> 00:12:48.768
- I know, I know. I know.

00:12:49.717 --> 00:12:51.772
And you are so good at it, look at you.

00:12:51.772 --> 00:12:53.039
- I know.

00:12:53.039 --> 00:12:56.425
And I reached a point senior
year where my adjustment

00:12:56.425 --> 00:12:57.258
felt like me.

00:12:57.258 --> 00:13:00.309
I had actually convinced
myself that I was this person

00:13:00.309 --> 00:13:04.226
and she, me, pretty,
talented but not stuck up.

00:13:07.038 --> 00:13:10.928
A girl who laughed a lot
at every stupid thing every

00:13:10.928 --> 00:13:15.095
boy said, and who lowered
her eyes at the right moment

00:13:17.614 --> 00:13:19.530
and deferred.

00:13:19.530 --> 00:13:21.363
Who learned to defer.

00:13:23.728 --> 00:13:25.435
When the boys took over the conversation.

00:13:25.435 --> 00:13:28.268
I really remember this so clearly.

00:13:30.496 --> 00:13:32.440
And I could tell it was
working and I was much less

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:35.133
annoying to the guys than I had been.

00:13:35.133 --> 00:13:37.773
They liked me better and I liked that.

00:13:37.773 --> 00:13:41.940
This was conscious but it was
at the same time motivated

00:13:42.833 --> 00:13:44.083
and fully felt.

00:13:45.296 --> 00:13:47.546
This was real, real acting.

00:13:51.342 --> 00:13:55.746
I got to Vassar which 43
years ago was a single sex

00:13:55.746 --> 00:13:58.278
institution like all the
colleges and what they called

00:13:58.278 --> 00:14:02.431
the Seven Sisters, the female Ivy League.

00:14:02.431 --> 00:14:05.679
And I made some very quick
but lifelong and challenging

00:14:05.679 --> 00:14:09.846
friends and with their help
outside of any competition

00:14:11.267 --> 00:14:13.434
for boys my brain woke up.

00:14:15.389 --> 00:14:17.639
(applause)

00:14:20.590 --> 00:14:24.757
I got up and I got outside
myself and I found myself

00:14:26.391 --> 00:14:27.224
again.

00:14:27.224 --> 00:14:30.627
I didn't have to pretend,
I could be goofy, vehement,

00:14:30.627 --> 00:14:34.477
aggressive, and slovenly, and
open, and funny, and tough

00:14:34.477 --> 00:14:36.672
and my friends let me.

00:14:36.672 --> 00:14:38.772
I didn't wash my hair
for three weeks once.

00:14:38.772 --> 00:14:40.855
(laughs)

00:14:41.751 --> 00:14:45.334
They accepted me like
the Velveteen Rabbit.

00:14:46.761 --> 00:14:50.928
I became real instead of
an imaginary stuffed bunny.

00:14:51.809 --> 00:14:54.240
This is your third parish in five years.

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:55.331
Why?

00:14:55.331 --> 00:14:56.390
- Call the pastor.

00:14:56.390 --> 00:14:57.737
Ask him why I left.

00:14:57.737 --> 00:14:58.936
It's perfectly innocent.

00:14:58.936 --> 00:15:00.755
- I'm not calling the pastor.

00:15:00.755 --> 00:15:01.837
- Okay, I'm a good priest.

00:15:01.837 --> 00:15:04.047
- Go after another child,
and another child until

00:15:04.047 --> 00:15:05.337
you are stopped.

00:15:05.337 --> 00:15:06.402
- What nun did you speak to?

00:15:06.402 --> 00:15:07.530
- I won't say.

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:08.776
- I've not touched a child!

00:15:08.776 --> 00:15:09.693
- You have!

00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:13.510
- You haven't the slightest
proof of anything.

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:15.416
- But I have my certainty.

00:15:15.416 --> 00:15:17.968
May I ask what you're writing
down? With that ballpoint

00:15:17.968 --> 00:15:18.801
pen?

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:23.531
- Oh, nothing it's an idea for a sermon.

00:15:23.531 --> 00:15:25.270
- Oh, you had one right now?

00:15:25.270 --> 00:15:27.592
- I get them all the time.

00:15:27.592 --> 00:15:28.774
- How fortunate.

00:15:28.774 --> 00:15:31.107
- Sister Aloysius is quite
an unpleasant character.

00:15:31.107 --> 00:15:32.675
How did you prepare for that role?

00:15:32.675 --> 00:15:36.148
- Yes, she is an unpleasant
character, but for me the

00:15:36.148 --> 00:15:37.981
process has to do with

00:15:40.768 --> 00:15:44.935
being her and being in her
life and I imagine events in

00:15:46.404 --> 00:15:49.458
her life that might have effected her.

00:15:49.458 --> 00:15:53.625
Events that led her to marry
early to get out of the house.

00:15:55.278 --> 00:15:59.445
Events that after her husband
died led her to the convent.

00:16:01.107 --> 00:16:03.184
I look at the time in which she lived.

00:16:03.184 --> 00:16:07.351
This takes place in 1964
when for very smart ambitious

00:16:08.727 --> 00:16:12.984
women, there were very
limited opportunities.

00:16:12.984 --> 00:16:14.491
This will not do.

00:16:14.491 --> 00:16:15.762
- It's just til June.

00:16:15.762 --> 00:16:18.066
- I'll throw your son out of this school.

00:16:18.066 --> 00:16:20.137
- Why would you do that if
it didn't start with him?

00:16:20.137 --> 00:16:22.446
- Because I will stop this.

00:16:22.446 --> 00:16:26.029
I had very brilliant women
teachers who now would not

00:16:26.029 --> 00:16:28.675
be teachers because the salary is too low.

00:16:28.675 --> 00:16:32.508
They'd be lawyers or
doctors or businesswomen.

00:16:33.993 --> 00:16:37.639
Just because those opportunities
weren't there for women

00:16:37.639 --> 00:16:38.737
then.

00:16:38.737 --> 00:16:43.640
So I imagined, I just imagined
the whole context of where

00:16:43.640 --> 00:16:46.640
she exists, how she came to be here.

00:16:47.749 --> 00:16:50.544
But I don't know how
anybody can possibly believe

00:16:50.544 --> 00:16:53.586
that I have less of a stake
in mothering that little

00:16:53.586 --> 00:16:55.669
boy than Mr. Kramer does.

00:17:00.797 --> 00:17:02.047
I'm his mother.

00:17:04.715 --> 00:17:06.316
I'm his mother.

00:17:06.316 --> 00:17:07.483
How thrilling!

00:17:08.494 --> 00:17:10.744
Not to have it be about me.

00:17:11.830 --> 00:17:13.913
(laughs)

00:17:15.314 --> 00:17:19.481
Charlie Kaufman's acceptance
speech which he just faxed.

00:17:22.286 --> 00:17:24.661
Thursday night February 20th.

00:17:24.661 --> 00:17:28.204
It's been suggested that I
write a speech in case I win

00:17:28.204 --> 00:17:29.605
the BAFTA Award.

00:17:29.605 --> 00:17:33.722
I've been told Meryl Streep
has been kind enough to offer

00:17:33.722 --> 00:17:37.070
to accept on my behalf should I win.

00:17:37.070 --> 00:17:40.748
I feel some pressure to
come up with a speech that

00:17:40.748 --> 00:17:43.156
Meryl won't mind reading aloud.

00:17:43.156 --> 00:17:45.903
(laughs)

00:17:45.903 --> 00:17:49.833
I feel pressure to give her
a joke, but simultaneously

00:17:49.833 --> 00:17:52.514
the great fear that I might
give her, inadvertently, a bad

00:17:52.514 --> 00:17:57.353
joke and it will bomb and it
will be as if I have spit on

00:17:57.353 --> 00:18:00.186
Meryl Streep from across the ocean

00:18:03.625 --> 00:18:06.038
and this after she has
been kind enough to offer

00:18:06.038 --> 00:18:07.920
to accept on my behalf.

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:10.253
This man is out of his mind.

00:18:12.342 --> 00:18:13.555
I don't want to do that.

00:18:13.555 --> 00:18:16.709
After all, she is just
great in adaptation, a truly

00:18:16.709 --> 00:18:18.469
amazing performance.

00:18:18.469 --> 00:18:20.552
(laughs)

00:18:21.448 --> 00:18:23.706
Oh god, I'm going to kill him.

00:18:23.706 --> 00:18:26.803
I should reiterate that
this is Charlie saying this

00:18:26.803 --> 00:18:30.945
not Meryl Streep who I
find exceedingly modest.

00:18:30.945 --> 00:18:34.424
Maybe one of the most
modest, nicest people ever.

00:18:34.424 --> 00:18:36.735
(laughs)

00:18:36.735 --> 00:18:38.385
Oh, I'm being set up.

00:18:38.385 --> 00:18:41.079
Again, that was me, Charlie.

00:18:41.079 --> 00:18:45.246
So, to be safe I'll keep it
simple and straightforward.

00:18:46.327 --> 00:18:49.756
I would like to thank BAFTA
for this wonderful honor.

00:18:49.756 --> 00:18:51.589
I would like to spank-

00:18:53.035 --> 00:18:55.118
(laughs)

00:19:04.677 --> 00:19:06.177
Thank Spike Jones.

00:19:07.093 --> 00:19:09.176
(laughs)

00:19:16.898 --> 00:19:19.261
I want to thank Meryl Streep
who I understand is here

00:19:19.261 --> 00:19:20.184
tonight.

00:19:20.184 --> 00:19:22.115
(laughs)

00:19:22.115 --> 00:19:24.584
Looks into the crowd,
he's giving me direction.

00:19:24.584 --> 00:19:29.234
Nic Cage, and Chris Cooper
whom I understand are not.

00:19:29.234 --> 00:19:30.695
I am not here either.

00:19:30.695 --> 00:19:32.603
I wish I were.

00:19:32.603 --> 00:19:34.059
Thank you very much.

00:19:34.059 --> 00:19:36.121
(applause)

00:19:36.121 --> 00:19:37.621
Oh boy, thank you.

00:19:41.923 --> 00:19:46.090
You know there are some
days when I myself think I'm

00:19:47.467 --> 00:19:48.903
overrated.

00:19:48.903 --> 00:19:50.986
(laughs)

00:19:59.191 --> 00:20:00.358
But not today.

00:20:04.948 --> 00:20:09.115
Now take back the soul of
Dennis George Finch Hutton.

00:20:10.613 --> 00:20:13.030
Whom you have shared with us.

00:20:15.287 --> 00:20:18.704
He brought us joy, and we loved him well.

00:20:24.126 --> 00:20:25.459
He was not ours.

00:20:30.349 --> 00:20:32.368
He was not mine.

00:20:32.368 --> 00:20:33.566
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:20:33.566 --> 00:20:36.812
I made this video because
Mai Taruto asked me to,

00:20:36.812 --> 00:20:38.471
so there's a famous
entreprenuer that you want me

00:20:38.471 --> 00:20:40.975
to profile next leave
it in the comments below

00:20:40.975 --> 00:20:42.055
and I'll see what I can do.

00:20:42.055 --> 00:20:44.218
I'd also love to know which
of Meryl Streep's rules

00:20:44.218 --> 00:20:46.506
meant the most to you,
what advice struck you the

00:20:46.506 --> 00:20:49.464
hardest, what impact it's
going to have on your life.

00:20:49.464 --> 00:20:50.346
Super curious to find out.

00:20:50.346 --> 00:20:52.625
Leave it in the comments
below and I will join

00:20:52.625 --> 00:20:53.689
in the discussion.

00:20:53.689 --> 00:20:56.553
Thank you so much for
watching, continue to believe

00:20:56.553 --> 00:20:58.386
and I'll see you soon.

